<p>Scholars disagree on when, exactly, Rome fell. But if you watch the new video from <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m7qqGQxfpGc">City 3D Timelapse</a> above, you’ll understand the lack of debate on the question of whether it fell. Around the turn of the sixth century AD, buildings start vanishing at a rate that soon turns alarming; long before the end of the first millennium, it’s clear that Rome has been plunged into very hard times indeed. In the video’s accelerated visual history, that much-scrutinized 500-year span passes in a little more than five seconds. Just one minute later, it has spread not just beyond the old city limits, but beyond the very frame of the aerial view, becoming the vast urbanized region we know today.</p>
<p>Legend has it that Rome was founded in 753 BC, when Romulus killed his twin brother Remus. As reflected by the video, archaeological evidence suggests that the area was settled earlier, though the traditional date of Rome’s founding roughly coincides with the emergence of what we know as the Roman Kingdom.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>There follow the Roman Republic, the Roman Empire, the medieval era — when the once-formidable Eternal City was left practically for dead&nbsp; — the Renaissance, the Roman Baroque period, the industrial era, and finally, our time of globalization. For reference, the classical archaeologist <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Battista_Piranesi">Giovanni Piranesi</a> went to Rome and drew his <a href="https://bcma.bowdoin.edu/antiquity/objects/vedute-di-roma-series/">influential etchings of its ruins</a> around the 1:55 mark of this three-minute video.</p>
<p>Not that the Rome captured in <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/363415">Piranesi’s&nbsp;<em>Vedute</em></a> bears much resemblance to the Rome of the twenty-first century, for better or for worse. You can still see the Colosseum by moonlight, but the ambience won’t be the same: <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2021/12/when-the-colosseum-in-rome-became-the-home-of-hundreds-of-exotic-plant-species.html">the overgrowth</a> has long since been scraped off, and the cargo shorts are out in force year-round. Regardless, no city is best appreciated through its landmarks alone, not even the modern Rome so long both celebrated and dismissed as an open-air museum. Watching it rise, fall, and rise again from above within the length of a Eurovision song reminds us that it wasn’t built, or rebuilt, in a day — and that, in the face of often-formidable difficulties, it even now manages to put up something new once in a while.</p>
